  <h2>5. Hardware, DDC/CI and risk</h2>

  <p>
    This is the section worth reading twice. MacQ works by writing to your monitor's
    own configuration over DDC/CI. That is the same mechanism the monitor's physical
    buttons use, but it is worth understanding what it means:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      DDC/CI is implemented differently by different manufacturers and models. A
      command that is harmless on one monitor may do nothing, or something
      unexpected, on another.
    </li>
    <li>
      MacQ is developed and tested against a limited set of hardware. Your monitor
      may respond differently.
    </li>
    <li>
      Changing an input source will disconnect the display you are currently looking
      at, which is the intended behaviour, but can leave you needing the monitor's
      physical buttons to get back.
    </li>
  </ul>

